  "issue_number": 10875,
  "issue_title": "Worker executes task after rejecting it when ETA timestamp conversion fails",
  "issue_body": "\n# Checklist\n\n<!--\nTo check an item on the list replace [ ] with [x].\n-->\n\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] This has already been asked to the [[discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions)](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n* [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [[contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n* [x] I have checked the [[issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n* [x] I have checked the [[pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n* [x] I have checked the [[commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n* [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n* [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [[pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html)](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n* [ ] I have included the output of `celery -A proj report` in the issue.\n  (if you are not able to do this, then at least specify the Celery\n  version affected).\n* [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n* [ ] I have included the contents of `pip freeze` in the issue.\n* [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n* [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n* [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n* None found.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n* None found.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: `5.7.0b1`\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```text\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.13.15\n            billiard:4.3.1 py-amqp:5.4.1\nplatform -> system:Windows arch:64bit, WindowsPE\n            kernel version:11 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.default.Loader\nsettings -> transport:amqp results:disabled\n\ndeprecated_settings: None\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\nThe issue was reproduced against the current `main` branch corresponding to\nCelery `5.7.0b1`.\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n* **Python**: 3.13.15\n* **Celery**: 5.7.0b1 / current `main`\n* **Kombu**: 5.7.0b1\n* **Billiard**: 4.3.1\n* **py-amqp**: 5.4.1\n* **OS**: Windows 11, 64-bit\n* **Broker**: The behavior can be reproduced with an in-process `memory://` broker; the `celery report` above shows the development environment's default AMQP transport.\n* **Result backend**: Not required\n\n### Python Packages\n\nThe relevant dependency versions from `celery report` are:\n\n```text\ncelery==5.7.0b1\nkombu==5.7.0b1\nbilliard==4.3.1\npy-amqp==5.4.1\nPython 3.13.15\n```\n\nA complete `pip freeze` can be added separately if required.\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\nNo external result backend is required.\n\nThe issue is in the worker's ETA handling in\n`celery/worker/strategy.py`.\n\nA `memory://` broker is sufficient to reproduce the control-flow issue.\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nThe problematic code path is:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    eta = to_timestamp(to_system_tz(req.eta))\nexcept (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n    error(...)\n    req.reject(requeue=False)\n```\n\nThere is no `return` after `req.reject(requeue=False)`.\n\nWhen ETA conversion raises `OverflowError` or `ValueError`, execution therefore\ncontinues into the normal task-processing path:\n\n```python\ntask_reserved(req)\nhandle(req)\n```\n\nFor example, with the worker timezone set to `Asia/Kolkata`, an ETA such as:\n\n```text\n9999-12-31T23:59:59+00:00\n```\n\ncan raise `OverflowError` during timezone conversion/timestamp conversion.\n\nThe observed behavior is:\n\n```text\nCouldn't convert ETA ...\nRequest.reject(requeue=False)\ntask_reserved(req)\nhandle(req)\n```\n\nThe task is consequently executed immediately even though its message has\nalready been rejected.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nWhen ETA conversion fails with `OverflowError` or `ValueError`, the worker\nshould reject the message and stop processing that request.\n\nAfter:\n\n```python\nreq.reject(requeue=False)\n```\n\nthe worker should not reserve or execute the task.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nThe worker rejects the message but then continues processing the same request.\n\nAs a result, the task can execute immediately despite having just been rejected\nwith `requeue=False`.\n\nThe problematic control flow is:\n\n```python\nexcept (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n    error(...)\n    req.reject(requeue=False)\n\n# execution incorrectly continues\ntask_reserved(req)\nhandle(req)\n```\n\nThe minimal fix is to return immediately after rejecting the request:\n\n```diff\n except (OverflowError, ValueError) as exc:\n     error(...)\n     req.reject(requeue=False)\n+    return\n```\n\nI reproduced the issue against the current `main` branch. The regression tests\nfail on the original implementation and pass with this control-flow fix.\n",
